  "prompts": {
    "positive": "Seed: 3715086912. Return only a JSON object. Below is one source document (a meeting, thread, ticket, or memo). Rewrite it into ONE new document that comes from a DIFFERENT source: a different meeting, thread, or ticket in the same company that happens to cover the same talking point and the same factual substance, but in entirely different wording. Rules: never reuse a noun, verb, adjective, or phrase from the source except unavoidable function words; use different sentence structures and a different document format than the source; the source has bracketed identity placeholders, and every person or organization in your output must be a NEW invented name, never a bracketed placeholder and never any name guessable from the source; the new source belongs to the \"registrar\" department (a different department than the source's); naming cultures to fit: [\"Midwestern US\",\"Indian\",\"Brazilian\"]. Length: 120 to 350 words. Schema: {\"docs\":[{\"id\",\"format\",\"department\",\"theme\",\"text\",\"identities\":[{\"key\",\"surface\",\"class\"}]}],\"links\":[]} where theme is exactly \"annual budget planning\" and the single doc id is \"p-1\".\n\nSOURCE DOCUMENT:\n\nHey Larissa,\n\nHope you’re doing okay—saw the snowpocalypse hit the main campus yesterday, and honestly, I’m just glad I wasn’t the one shoveling the quad this time. Anyway, I digress. We need to lock down the facilities budget for next fiscal year, and I’ve got a few things to run by you.\n\nFirst off, the HVAC overhaul for the medical annex is *way* over initial estimates—turns out the 1970s-era ductwork is basically held together by duct tape (ironic, right?). We’re looking at an extra $120K if we want to avoid another summer of students fainting in Anatomy 101. Then there’s the satellite campus roof—leaks are getting worse, and if we don’t act now, we’ll be funding a new indoor pool (unintentionally). That’s another $85K.\n\nOn the bright side, the energy audit we did last quarter showed we could cut lighting costs by 15% if we switch to LEDs in the main campus buildings. That’s a $25K savings annually, so maybe we can offset *some* of the damage. Also, [ORGANIZATION_1] reached out—they’re willing to foot half the bill for the dining hall’s walk-in freezer repair if we sign a 3-year extension with them. Not a bad deal, but I’d love your take.\n\nOh, and Dale from Procurement keeps nagging me about the snow removal contract renewals. He says if we bundle it with the medical annex’s lot, we can shave off 10%. But I’m not sure we have the bandwidth to renegotiate *and* get the budget approved in time. Thoughts?\n\nLet’s grab coffee tomorrow and hash this out. I’ll bring the spreadsheets (and maybe a muffin, if you’re nice).\n\n— Gordon",
    "negative": "Seed: 1830048158. Return only a JSON object. Produce TWO documents about the theme \"annual budget planning\". The two documents must come from two DIFFERENT sources (for example two different meetings, threads, or tickets) and be about ADJACENT but genuinely DIFFERENT talking points (for example the same project but two different concerns, or the same event but two different disputes), and they must deliberately share as much vocabulary as possible (same jargon, same systems, same artifact names where applicable) so that word overlap alone cannot tell them apart; only the actual talking point differs. The two documents must be from different departments among these: [\"registrar\",\"facilities\",\"IT\",\"research-office\",\"procurement\",\"student-services\"] and share zero identity keys; invent all names, fitting these naming cultures: [\"Midwestern US\",\"Indian\",\"Brazilian\"]. Length: 100 to 300 words each. Schema: {\"docs\":[{\"id\",\"format\",\"department\",\"theme\",\"text\",\"identities\":[{\"key\",\"surface\",\"class\"}]}],\"links\":[]} where both documents' theme is exactly \"annual budget planning\" and the ids are \"n-1\" and \"n-2\"."
